<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">UNITA</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as National Union for the Total Independence of Angola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">UNITA, the National Union for the Total Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, is an Angolan movement founded in 1966 by Jonas Savimbi, with its strongest support among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a> of the central highlands. It fought the Portuguese and then, with South African and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> support, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> government in the Angolan civil war. After Savimbi was killed in 2002 a peace agreement ended the war, and UNITA became the main opposition party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/unita.jpg" alt="UNITA" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ceresnet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_UNITA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jonas Savimbi and António da Costa Fernandes founded UNITA on 13 March 1966 at Muangai in Moxico province, in Portuguese Angola, with some 200 other delegates present. It launched its first attack on the Portuguese colonial authorities on 25 December 1966. Savimbi had originally been affiliated with Holden Roberto's National Liberation Front of Angola (FNLA), and UNITA fought alongside the FNLA and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> in the war for independence that lasted from 1961 to 1975. Its leadership came heavily from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a>, Angola's majority ethnic group. Its policies were at first Maoist, and it received aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> until 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Portuguese withdrew in 1974 and 1975, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> and UNITA clashed and a civil war began that lasted until 2002. The MPLA leader Agostinho Neto became the first president. Backed by Soviet and Cuban money, weapons and troops, the MPLA defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> militarily and forced it largely into exile. UNITA was nearly destroyed in November 1975 but survived and set up a second government at Huambo. It recovered with South African aid and was strengthened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> support in the 1980s. The MPLA was strongest in the cities, the coast and the oil fields, while UNITA held much of the interior highlands. Until 1996 it was funded through diamond mines in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwango-river">Cuango River</a> valley.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> withdrew its troops in 1989. After the Bicesse Accords of 1991 elections were held in 1992 with Savimbi and José Eduardo dos Santos as presidential candidates. When no one won an overall majority in the first round and UNITA questioned the vote, fighting resumed in October 1992. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> imposed an embargo on UNITA, and the Lusaka Protocol of 1994 sought a government of national unity. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a> offensive in 1999, Operation Restore, ended UNITA as a conventional army. Both sides were criticised for human rights abuses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Savimbi was killed in an ambush on 22 February 2002. Six weeks later, in April 2002, UNITA agreed to a ceasefire, and about 350,000 soldiers and family members were gathered in 33 demobilisation camps. In August 2002 UNITA formally gave up its armed wing, and it has since taken part in electoral politics as a party. Isaías Samakuva, elected to lead it after Savimbi's immediate successor António Dembo died, was in turn succeeded by Adalberto Costa Júnior in 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “UNITA” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalist revolt began in 1961, with the MPLA, FNLA and <b>UNITA</b> fighting Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a></li></ul></section>
